  /* Five across. A SCOPED COMPOUND rather than anything left to a page sheet,
     for the reason ProcessStepsBlock.astro:734-750 records for its own
     `.cols-5`: both pages that draw five spell it at (0,2,0)
     (`.smilemakeover .issues-grid`, smile-makeover.css:363-366;
     `.singletooth .benefits-grid`, single-tooth-dental-implants.css:563-566),
     and this file's scoped `.why-grid` compiles to `.why-grid[data-astro-cid-…]`
     — also (0,2,0). A tie decided by bundle order is not a thing a band's
     column count should hang on. This compound is (0,3,0) and wins outright.

     18px, matching BOTH sheets exactly — they agree on the desktop shape even
     though they disagree about the collapse below. */
  .why-grid.cols-5 {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
    gap: 18px;
  }

  /* THE FIVE-TRACK LADDER, AND THE ONE PLACE THE TWO PAGES DISAGREE.
     Stated here rather than folded into the 991px/780px pair below, because a
     five-track row is already under 220px per card at 1240px and has to start
     collapsing before three or four do.

       smile-makeover.css:691,713,724   5 -> 3 @1100 -> 2 @780 -> 1 @480
       single-tooth…css:674,687,…       5 -> 3 @1240 -> 2 @1100 -> 1 @780

     Neither is wrong; they are two pages authored a year apart. This takes
     smile-makeover's, which is EXACT for that route and never overflows on the
     other — single-tooth draws five where its sheet drew three between 1100 and
     1240, and two where its sheet drew one between 480 and 780. Recorded on
     that block rather than papered over, and the honest close is a named ladder
     field the way `collapse_at` closed the same species of disagreement for
     service_cards. Not worth a second field for two rows today.

     All three are (0,3,0), same as the desktop rule, so SOURCE ORDER decides
     between them — narrowest last. Reordering these three blocks changes the
     layout. */
  @media (max-width: 1100px) {
